-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.0 (2026-11-02)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * First release (ORDER 350; Daniel, 2026-11-01: "nothing we do should silently fail or have a fall back -
    either it works or not and the user must know"; 2026-11-02: "Why machinery to keep a failure for each?"):
    one engine for every package of the edition that has to say what failed, why and what to do, kept until it
    is repaired, in place of a store, commands and a notifier in each package
  * sparky-stereo-problems: keep, run, clear, say, list [--json], repair, hide and show; the machine's problems in
    /var/lib/sparky-stereo-problems, this boot's in /run/sparky-stereo-problems, the person's own in
    $XDG_STATE_HOME/sparky-stereo-problems; one record per file, written through a dot-file and renamed under a
    lock; a problem kept again with the same message keeps its first time and its hide
  * The same engine as a C library (private, with a header and a pkg-config file) and a Python module
  * Messages are each package's own translations built into journal message catalogs (catalog, used at package
    build time), so journalctl -x explains each problem too; the engine reads them in gettext's order
    (LANGUAGE, LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, LANG, /etc/default/locale) and mirrors every event to the journal with the
    message's MESSAGE_ID
  * Every failed system service is told: a top-level service.d drop-in names sparky-stereo-problems-failed@ in
    OnFailure= (systemd.unit(5)), which keeps the service's own last lines with systemd's restart as the repair
  * Repairs of the machine's problems through pkexec and one polkit action,
    org.sparkylinux.sparkystereo.problems.repair, which runs only repair of what root kept
  * The record keeps its English text (text_en, shown when no catalog has its message any more), the package's own
    fields (data) and further sentences (more, what to do); a repair whose command keeps the problem again is not a
    repair; clear --all PACKAGE at its removal, and the problems of a package that is not installed any more go
  * The catalog builder reads gettext .po files too (CONTEXT|MSGID, %1..%26 in any order); text, a shell caller's
    way to say one kept problem; the command's own texts in the nine languages; the command says a kept problem on
    standard error unless --quiet
  * Python: Steps, the problems.py commands of the edition's packages answered by the engine, with migrate
  * Running out of memory is said like every failure (required-languages; ORDER 350 milestone 2c): LOCAL64 from
    the engine's catalogs, in the person's language and in English, rendered with memory of its own; a wrong call
    of the library (a scope that is none, a missing name, arguments or details it cannot read) is said too, never
    a bare return. Steps.migrate keeps a record that cannot move as one problem of the package (old-store), with
    each record and what failed, until a repair has moved them all
